Job Description 12
Responsibilities:
- Execute structural analysis, blast modeling, and structural hardening design for high-consequence facilities, government complexes, data centers, and defense installations.
- Perform single-degree-of-freedom (SDOF) and multi-degree-of-freedom (MDOF) dynamic response analyses under external blast overpressure and impulse loads.
- Evaluate structural components under high-strain-rate material behavior, dynamic increase factors (DIF), and ductility/rotational limits.
- Design reinforced concrete blast walls, hardened structural steel frames, blast-resistant door/window connections, and anti-ram physical vehicle barriers.
- Conduct progressive collapse vulnerability assessments using alternate load path methods (ALPM) following sudden column loss scenarios.
- Coordinate with security risk consultants, physical blast threat modelers, and architectural specialists to integrate CPTED (Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design) principles.
Minimum Qualifications:
- Master’s or Bachelor’s degree in Structural Engineering.
- 5+ years of experience in protective design, blast-resistant engineering, and physical security structural hardening.
- Proficiency in advanced dynamic/blast analysis tools such as LS-DYNA, ABAQUS, SBEDS, WinBLAST, or SAP2000.
- Deep familiarity with protective design criteria including UFC 4-010-01 (DoD Minimum Antiterrorism Standards), UFC 3-340-02 (Structures to Resist the Effects of Accidental Explosions), ASCE 59, and GSA Progressive Collapse Guidelines.
- Strong foundation in non-linear dynamic mechanics, plastic hinge development, and high-energy structural absorption.
Preferred Qualifications:
- Experience performing explicit non-linear finite element blast-structure interaction simulations in LS-DYNA.
- Active security clearance or prior project experience on embassy and critical national infrastructure projects.
- Knowledge of ballistic impact mitigation and forced entry structural design criteria (ASTM F3038 / DOS SD-STD-01.01).

