Job Description 14
Responsibilities:
- Perform structural analysis, modeling, and detailed engineering for light gauge steel framing (LGSF) / cold-formed steel (CFS) structures (modular housing, commercial framing, and curtain wall studs).
- Design cold-formed C-sections, Z-purlins, hat channels, floor joists, and prefabricated shear wall panel assemblies.
- Evaluate local buckling, distortional buckling, and global flexural-torsional buckling using the Direct Strength Method (DSM) and Effective Width Method.
- Design self-drilling screw connections, bolted clip angles, hold-downs, strap bracing, and track-to-foundation anchorages.
- Generate automated production panel drawings, cut lists, and machine-readable CNC framing files for roll-forming machinery.
- Coordinate with architectural, MEP, and modular manufacturing teams to integrate utility chases, service cutouts, and acoustic/fire assemblies.
Minimum Qualifications:
- Bachelor’s degree in Civil or Structural Engineering.
- 2–5 years of structural design experience focused on cold-formed steel (CFS) / light gauge steel construction.
- High proficiency in CFS structural design software such as Vertex BD, CFS (RSG Software), Simpson Strong-Tie CFS Designer, FRAMECAD, or StrucSoft MWF.
- Strong knowledge of cold-formed steel design specifications including AISI S100, AISI S240, AISI S400, Eurocode 3 Part 1-3 (EN 1993-1-3), and IS 801.
- Solid understanding of lateral load-resisting systems using strap-braced panels and steel sheet sheathing.
Preferred Qualifications:
- Experience with industrialized modular volumetric pod assembly design.
- Familiarity with BIM workflows and parametric modeling in Revit or SolidWorks.
- Hands-on knowledge of cold-roll forming manufacturing tolerances and automated production machinery.

